      "rev_n_2_28f773_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_28f773_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_28f773",
        "thought_text": "Instead of using the Chinese Remainder Theorem, consider using modular arithmetic properties to determine if it's possible to achieve the desired congruence. Specifically, check if there exists a multiple of A that, when added to some other multiples of A, results in a sum congruent to C modulo B. This involves checking if C is a multiple of the greatest common divisor (GCD) of A and B, and if the necessary conditions for achieving the congruence are met.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_28f773_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_e3dc7f7c14",
        "created_at": 1794527410.9413373
      },
      "rev_n_1_654277_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_654277_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_654277",
        "thought_text": "Thought-1: Check if there exists a multiple of A that, when added to C, results in a sum divisible by B. This can be done by checking if (C + k*A) % B == 0 for some non-negative integer k.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_654277_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_c2e2fd7b60",
        "created_at": 1794527425.3012192
      },
      "rev_n_3_e1a56b_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_e1a56b_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_e1a56b",
        "thought_text": "Thought-1: Generate a sequence of multiples of A and check for a sum that satisfies the congruence, while also considering the special case where C is 0.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
